  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:transition-length",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "There exists \\(K_\\alpha<\\infty\\), depending only on \\(\\alpha,c,C_0,\\mu,B,r_0,\\sigma\\), such that for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), \\(\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal P_n}\\mathbb E_P[\\operatorname{length}(I_n^{\\mathrm{out}})]/w_n\\le K_\\alpha\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Kalpha",
      "alpha",
      "c",
      "C0",
      "mu",
      "B",
      "r0",
      "sigma",
      "n",
      "P",
      "calP",
      "Iout",
      "w"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:transition-scale",
      "def:analytic-interval",
      "lem:conditional-reduction",
      "lem:gaussian-outer-inversion"
    ],
    "justification": "Coverage alone could be achieved by projection; transition-order expected length makes the interval informative at joint zero.",
    "gap": "Neither the panel anchor nor generic minimum-distance theory proves a donor-uniform expected-length bound at the sum of first- and second-order scales.",
    "consumer": "The bound supplies a principled uncertainty floor for entrywise treatment-effect reporting in strong-factor panels.",
    "proof_tex": "On \\(G_n\\), \\(h_n\\le2/(cn)\\), and the span representations give\n\\[\n s_D(m_n)\n \\le {h_n\\over\\sigma}(\\|u\\|_2+\\|v\\|_2)\n \\le {2(\\|u\\|_2+\\|v\\|_2)\\over c\\sigma n}.\n\\]\nThe Gaussian-outer-inversion expectation bound, followed by the lower bound for \\(w_n\\) proved in \\(\\textnormal{lem:conditional-reduction}\\), therefore gives\n\\[\n \\mathbb E_P[2\\sigma^2W_n\\mathbf1\\{G_n\\}]\n \\le K_\\alpha {\\|u\\|_2+\\|v\\|_2+1\\over n}\n \\le K_\\alpha w_n.\n\\]\nThe same lemma gives \\(\\mathbb E_P[2d_n\\mathbf1\\{G_n\\}]=o(w_n)\\) uniformly.  For sufficiently large \\(n\\), the event \\(\\|E\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le3\\sigma\\sqrt n\\) is contained in \\(G_n\\), so\n\\[\n P(G_n^c)\\le e^{-n/2}.\n\\]\nSince the off-gate length is \\(2M_0\\) and \\(w_n\\ge\\sigma^2/(C_0n)\\),\n\\[\n {2M_0P(G_n^c)\\over w_n}\n \\le {2M_0C_0n\\over\\sigma^2}e^{-n/2}=o(1)\n\\]\nuniformly over the class.  Adding the three contributions proves the displayed uniform bound after enlarging \\(K_\\alpha\\).  This also shows explicitly why the bounded spectral fallback does not alter the transition order.",
